If `dateStr` is something like `2026-13-45` (invalid date), `Date.UTC(2026, 12, 45, ...)` in JavaScript rolls over: month 12 becomes month 0 of the next year, etc. So it would produce a valid-looking date. That's a data quality issue but not a security issue.

If `dateStr` is something completely malformed, like an injection string, each `Number()` call would produce `NaN`, and `Date.UTC(NaN, ...)` would produce `NaN` — an invalid date. This would be stored as an invalid timestamp or cause an error.

